; the metallurgy and corrosion chemistry of steel in construction.
; 
; PRODUCTION: iron ore → pig iron → steel
; iron ore (Fe2O3, Fe3O4) + coke (C) + limestone → blast furnace
; 2Fe2O3 + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO2    (reduction at 1500°C)
; pig iron: ~4% C, brittle. basic oxygen furnace removes carbon.
; steel: 0.02-2.1% C. carbon atoms in interstitial sites → harder.
; alloying: Cr (stainless), Ni (toughness), Mn (hardness), V (high-strength).
; 
; CORROSION: rusting (electrochemical reaction)
; anode: Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻       (iron dissolves)
; cathode: O2 + 2H2O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻  (oxygen reduced)
; Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)2 → Fe2O3·nH2O  (rust, hydrated iron oxide)
; requires: iron + oxygen + water (all three must be present).
; in concrete: alkaline pore water (pH 12-13) passivates steel → no rust.
; carbonation: CO2 + Ca(OH)2 → CaCO3 → pH drops → passivation breaks → rust.
; chlorides: penetrate passivation layer → accelerated corrosion (coastal buildings).
; 
; PROTECTION:
; galvanising: zinc coat. Zn oxidises preferentially (sacrificial anode).
; painting: barrier to O2 and H2O.
; stainless steel: Cr2O3 passive film self-repairs.
; concrete cover: alkaline environment passivates rebar.
; 
; WELDABILITY:
; low carbon steel: easily welded. high carbon: brittle heat-affected zone.
; carbon equivalent CE = C + Mn/6 + (Cr+Mo+V)/5 + (Ni+Cu)/15
; CE < 0.4: good weldability. CE > 0.7: preheat required.

